{"reference":"Amos 4","book":"Amos","chapter":4,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the Chapter Carefully","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The address to the “cows of Bashan” in verses 1–3 should not be reused as a misogynistic insult or body-shaming label, nor should it obscure men’s participation and institutional power in Samaria’s exploitation. Likewise, the critique of sacrifices and public offerings in verses 4–5 targets worship joined to exploitation; it is not a rejection of ritual, music, temple worship, or Jewish practice as such. The chapter’s hunger, drought, crop damage, violence, and devastation connect human injustice with a destabilized creation, but they do not authorize blaming particular disaster victims for particular sins. Its concluding praise of the God who forms mountains and creates wind joins creation and judgment in the book’s final form, though the origin and placement of such passages remain disputed. Modern Jewish, Black, civil-rights, liberation, womanist, and ecological readings can illuminate real dimensions of Amos, while remaining distinct, situated readings rather than a single voice.","verse_refs":["Amos 4:1-13","Amos 4:1-5","Amos 4:12-13","Amos 4:4-5","Amos 4:6-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_amos_004_4e055a393563c85e604d18b4"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}